{"id":"neet-2023-q074","pn":74,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"States of Matter","st":"Intermolecular forces","q":"Intermolecular forces are forces of attraction and repulsion between interacting particles that will include :\nA. dipole-dipole forces.\nB. dipole-induced dipole forces.\nC. hydrogen bonding.\nD. covalent bonding.\nE. dispersion forces.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["A, C, D, E are correct.","B, C, D, E are correct.","A, B, C, D are correct.","A, B, C, E are correct."],"c":3,"s":"Intermolecular forces act between molecules.\nCovalent bonds hold atoms together inside a molecule, so D is not included.\nDipole-dipole, dipole-induced dipole, hydrogen bonding and dispersion forces are intermolecular.\nSo A, B, C and E are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q056","pn":56,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"States of Matter","st":"Dalton's law of partial pressures","q":"Which one is not correct mathematical equation for Dalton’s Law of partial pressure? Here p = total pressure of gaseous mixture. (where pᵢ = partial pressure of ith gas, χᵢ = mole fraction of ith gas in gaseous mixture, p°ᵢ = pressure of ith gas in pure state)","o":["p = n₁(RT)/(V) + n₂(RT)/(V) + n₃(RT)/(V)","pᵢ = χᵢp","pᵢ = χᵢp°ᵢ","p = p₁ + p₂ + p₃"],"c":2,"s":"Dalton's law: the total pressure of non-reacting gases is the sum of the partial pressures, p = p₁ + p₂ + p₃.\nAlso pᵢ = χᵢp.\nUsing p = nRT/V for each gas gives p = (n₁ + n₂ + n₃)RT/V.\nSo options 1, 2 and 4 are correct.\nOption 3, pᵢ = χᵢp°ᵢ, is not part of Dalton's law (it is Raoult's law form)."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q085","pn":85,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"States of Matter","st":"Liquefaction and critical temperature","q":"Four gas cylinders containing He, N₂, CO₂ and NH₃ gases separately are gradually cooled from a temperature of 500 K. Which gas will liquify first ?\n(Given Tc in K - He : 5.3, N₂ : 126, CO₂ : 304.1 and NH₃ : 405.5)","o":["NH₃","He","N₂","CO₂"],"c":0,"s":"A gas can be liquefied only below its critical temperature.\nSo the gas with the highest critical temperature liquefies first on cooling.\nNH₃ has the highest Tc (405.5 K), so it liquefies first."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q087","pn":87,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"States of Matter","st":"Ideal gas equation","q":"A 10.0 L flask contains 64 g of oxygen at 27ºC. (Assume O₂ gas is behaving ideally). The pressure inside the flask in bar is (Given R = 0.0831 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","o":["498.6","49.8","4.9","2.5"],"c":2,"s":"PV = nRT, so P = nRT/V.\nn = 64/32 = 2 mol, T = 27 + 273 = 300 K.\nP = (2 × 0.0831 × 300)/(10) ≈ 4.9 bar."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q091","pn":91,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"States of Matter","st":"Boyle's law","q":"A vessel contains 3.2 g of dioxygen gas at STP (273.15 K and 1 atm pressure). The gas is now transferred to another vessel at constant temperature, where pressure becomes one third of the original pressure. The volume of new vessel in L is:\n(Given - molar volume at STP is 22.4 L)","o":["67.2","6.72","2.24","22.4"],"c":1,"s":"Moles of O₂ = 3.2/32 = 0.1 mol.\nVolume at STP = 0.1 × 22.4 = 2.24 L.\nBoyle's law: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.\n1 × 2.24 = (1/3) × V₂.\nV₂ = 6.72 L."}
